<mujoco model="so101_pick_place_scene">
  <!-- The real bench pick-and-place task: blue 2 cm cube -> white open box.
       Copy of robot_cube_scene.xml's setup (robot on a groundplane, no
       table geometry -- see robot_bare_scene.xml for why), with the objects
       sized and placed to match the physical bench on 2026-09-11.

       SIZES -- measured by hand, 2026-09-11:
         cube  2.0 cm, blue PLA
         box   14.0 cm wide x 8.5 cm deep x 4.9 cm tall, open top, white
               (wall thickness not measured; 3 mm assumed from the photo)

       POSITIONS -- as of 2026-09-16, these come from the RECORDING's own
       forward kinematics, not the ruler. History, because both numbers
       matter:

       On 2026-09-11 the ruler said cube 22.5 cm / box 32 cm out from the
       base's front edge, but real_to_sim_joint_mapping.py's rescale had no
       correction for this arm's two off-centre calibrations yet, and the
       recording's FK landed the grasp ~10 cm short of the ruler cube
       (docs/SESSION_2026-09-11_HANDOFF.md section 2).

       That same day, two per-joint zero offsets were fitted from table
       touches (JOINT_ZERO_OFFSET_DEG in real_sim_joint_mapping.py:
       shoulder_lift -7.5 deg, wrist_flex -14 deg -- this arm's wrist and
       shoulder both calibrate with less travel than 15 other SO-101 arms
       NVIDIA measured, which moves LeRobot's mid-travel "0%" off-centre).
       Re-running the SAME recording's FK through the corrected mapping
       closes most of the gap: the grasp point moves from 10 cm short to
       5 cm short of the ruler cube.

       5 cm is still real, and is the reach/gear-slack residual the
       project has not fully explained (CLAUDE.md, TELEOP_SESSION_LOG).
       Rather than split the difference, this scene places the cube and
       box at the CORRECTED RECORDING's own jaw position -- so replaying
       recordings/teleop_ref_pick_place.csv (--source real, sim-only, no
       hardware needed) shows the jaws actually closing around the cube
       and opening over the box, which is the useful property for
       checking a replay by eye. It will NOT exactly match a ruler
       measurement of the real bench until the residual is found.

       Recomputed 2026-09-16 (scripts/digital_twin_env/real_sim_mapping_test
       /real_sim_joint_mapping.py's real_to_sim_vector, applied to the
       recording's *_real_norm columns -- NOT the *_real_sim_deg columns,
       which were baked with no offsets at record time).

       WHICH GRIPPER GEOM TO MEASURE FROM -- a real bug, found and fixed
       the same day. The first attempt averaged fixed_jaw_box1 and
       moving_jaw_box1 ("jaw-mid"): x=+0.249 y=-0.012 z=0.084. That z is
       WRONG -- box1 is a coarse collision box near the BASE of each
       finger (so101.xml local z=-0.022 on the gripper body), not the tip.
       The actual pinch point is fixed_jaw_sph_tip1-3 / moving_jaw_sph_tip1-3
       (local z=-0.098 to -0.101, i.e. 7-8 cm further out along the
       finger) -- co-located with the gripperframe site (so101.xml defines
       it at local z=-0.098127, which is the tell). Recomputed from the
       TIP geoms and cross-checked against the site (they agree to the mm):
         grasp    t=29.9-30.8 s, gripper closed to 11.0 (holding the cube)
                  tip midpoint x=+0.239  y=-0.012  z=0.015
                  site (cross-check)  x=+0.250  y=-0.012  z=0.016
                  tip-to-tip gap at this closing: 1.91 cm -- a snug fit for
                  a 2.0 cm cube, physically sensible for a real grasp
         release  t=33.2-33.8 s, gripper opening (11.0 -> 27+) over the box
                  tip midpoint x=+0.364  y=+0.033  z=0.119
                  site (cross-check)  x=+0.369  y=+0.034  z=0.125

       Cube centre placed at the grasp tip midpoint xy (z dropped to
       0.010, resting on the floor -- the tip midpoint's own z, 0.015, is
       within 5 mm of that, i.e. consistent with a cube sitting on the
       floor). Box centred under the release tip midpoint xy.

       DOES A PLAIN REPLAY ACTUALLY LIFT THE CUBE? Tested with real
       physics (replay_pick_place.py steps mj_step through the whole
       recording, not just kinematics) -- NO, not reliably. Best case
       (scripts/digital_twin_env/robot_pick_place_test's dev history,
       2026-09-16) got both fingertip SPHERES (radius 0.75 mm each)
       touching the cube simultaneously for about 4 ticks (~0.07 s) with
       0.14 cm of lift, then it slipped. This is NOT the cube-position
       problem the earlier "10 cm short" finding was -- xy is now right
       to within about 1 cm, which used to be considered close. It looks
       instead like real teleoperated grasps rely on backlash/compliance/
       timing a bare kinematic trajectory replay does not reproduce, and
       the actual contact surfaces here are three 0.75 mm points per side,
       which is an unforgiving target. This matches the project's existing
       finding that a SCRIPTED grasp (grasp_cube.py's open-before-
       descending approach) is a different, more reliable thing than
       replaying a recorded trajectory -- see that script's header.

       USE THIS SCENE FOR: confirming the recorded reach and floor height
       line up with the objects by eye (they do, closely, now). NOT for:
       expecting replay_pick_place.py to show the cube actually lifted and
       carried into the box -- it currently does not, and that is reported
       (not silently assumed) by that script.

       ORIENTATION ASSUMED: the box's 14 cm side runs left-right across the
       arm's reach (y), 8.5 cm along it (x) -- how it sits in the bench
       photo, taken from the robot's side. -->

  <compiler angle="radian" autolimits="true" meshdir="../../../so101_assets/assets"/>

  <include file="../../../so101_assets/so101.xml"/>

  <visual>
    <headlight diffuse="0.6 0.6 0.6" ambient="0.3 0.3 0.3" specular="0 0 0"/>
    <rgba haze="0.15 0.25 0.35 1"/>
    <global azimuth="160" elevation="-25"/>
    <quality shadowsize="4096"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture type="skybox" builtin="gradient" rgb1="0.3 0.5 0.7"
             rgb2="0 0 0" width="512" height="3072"/>
    <!-- Dark glossy wood, like the bench tabletop. Two near-identical
         browns: enough variation to give the eye a depth cue, not enough
         to read as a checkerboard. -->
    <texture type="2d" name="wood" builtin="checker"
             rgb1="0.34 0.19 0.09" rgb2="0.32 0.18 0.085"
             width="512" height="512"/>
    <material name="wood" texture="wood" texuniform="true"
              texrepeat="10 10" reflectance="0.25" specular="0.4" shininess="0.6"/>
    <material name="cube_mat" rgba="0.08 0.20 0.85 1" specular="0.3" shininess="0.3"/>
    <material name="box_mat" rgba="0.94 0.94 0.92 1" specular="0.1"/>
  </asset>

  <default>
    <default class="cube_collision">
      <!-- Same contact settings as robot_cube_scene.xml -- see its comment
           for why condim=6 and why the jaws' params win the grasp contact. -->
      <geom contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="6"
            friction="0.8 0.02 0.001" solref="0.01 1" solimp="0.95 0.99 0.001"/>
    </default>
    <default class="box_wall">
      <geom type="box" contype="1" conaffinity="1" condim="3"
            friction="0.6 0.005 0.0001" material="box_mat"/>
    </default>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 1.5" dir="0 0 -1" directional="true"/>
    <geom name="floor" size="0 0 0.05" type="plane" material="wood"/>

    <!-- Straight down over the cube and the box together. -->
    <camera name="overhead" pos="0.30 0.01 0.80" xyaxes="0 1 0 -1 0 0"/>

    <!-- Roughly where the bench photo was taken from: behind the cube,
         looking out toward the box. -->
    <camera name="bench_view" pos="0.08 -0.01 0.28" mode="targetbody" target="pick_box"/>

    <!-- Wide angle on the whole arm plus both objects. -->
    <camera name="workspace" pos="0.85 -0.70 0.60" mode="targetbody" target="pick_box"/>

    <!-- The cube. 2 cm, 3.2 g (density 400 kg/m^3, same as robot_cube_scene).
         Sits on the floor at the recording's own grasp FINGERTIP midpoint
         xy (not the ruler's, not the earlier box1-based point -- see the
         header). Free joint -> qpos[6:13], right after the arm's 6 joints. -->
    <body name="cube" pos="0.239 -0.012 0.0100">
      <freejoint name="cube_freejoint"/>
      <geom name="cube_geom" type="box" size="0.010 0.010 0.010"
            mass="0.0032" material="cube_mat" class="cube_collision"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The box. Static (no joint): it does not move on the real bench
         either. Outer 8.5 (x) x 14.0 (y) x 4.9 (z) cm, 3 mm walls and floor.
         Half-extents: x 0.0425, y 0.070, z 0.0245. Centred under the
         recording's own release FINGERTIP midpoint xy (see header). -->
    <body name="pick_box" pos="0.364 0.033 0">
      <geom name="box_bottom" class="box_wall" size="0.0425 0.0700 0.0015" pos="0 0 0.0015"/>
      <geom name="box_wall_near" class="box_wall" size="0.0015 0.0700 0.0245" pos="-0.0410 0 0.0245"/>
      <geom name="box_wall_far" class="box_wall" size="0.0015 0.0700 0.0245" pos="0.0410 0 0.0245"/>
      <geom name="box_wall_left" class="box_wall" size="0.0425 0.0015 0.0245" pos="0 0.0685 0.0245"/>
      <geom name="box_wall_right" class="box_wall" size="0.0425 0.0015 0.0245" pos="0 -0.0685 0.0245"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
